# Chroma content pack, Politics, Elections and Public Office (modern Earth first, with the tribal and magic worlds).
# PART 2 of the pack's moments: LIFE ON THE MAIN RUNGS, an everyday pair per title on the road to office that only its
# holder meets (holds:): the party member, the local councillor, the campaign organiser, the political adviser, the
# mayor, the member of parliament, the minister, the party leader and the head of government.
# Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: chroma-library/library-spec.md. Brief: chroma-packs/politics/MOMENTS-BRIEF.md
# (part 2). Titles and perks: chroma-packs/politics/catalogue.py; their forms in the other worlds: worlds.py. Each pair's
# first moment grows from the brief's seed or the title's turning point, the second is another ordinary week of the role.
# No real party, country, leader or side of a real debate: the cause, the bill, the other side. Wrongs and honest acts
# are spread over every color. Pair halves share stages, ages, holds: and rate; calls split the colors 2 and 3, and both
# halves use the cycle named on the pair's header line. No option here takes a new title (holder pairs come back many
# times in a career; the engine's rule from the Science test): where the brief seeded a title step, the option carries
# aims: instead, and drops: stays on the few acts that truly end a role. Every moment has earth:, tribal: and magic: lines
# and an untagged tribal and magic scene; an option whose act reads differently in a world carries its own tribal: or
# magic: wording; the works and fails lines are written to read in every world.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- party member: calls WB / calls URG (cycle s3)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== the branch meeting runs late
tier: everyday
rate: 0.001
gap: 5-10
times: per party member a year: an active member sits through a long branch meeting several times a year, though fewer than half of members ever attend one; about 6 lives in 100 belong to a party at some point (catalogue share; estimate)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-95
calls: WB
holds: party member
tone: mixed
life: community
horizon: moment
roles: boss, colleague
earth: a branch meeting above a pub runs late: a motion on the local hospital, and two hours of procedure before anyone reaches it
tribal: the faction's elders talk late at the fire, and the matter of the healer's lodge waits behind every old quarrel
magic: the faction's ward meeting in a back room of the guild hall runs late, and the motion on the infirmary is still not reached
scene: Nineteen members sit in the room above the pub, and the motion on the local hospital is item eleven. At half past nine {boss}, who chairs the branch, is still going through the minutes of the last meeting line by line.
scene W: Every amendment has to be moved, seconded and put to the vote, and {N} knows the standing orders say so for good reasons. It is still only item four.
scene U: {N} has read the hospital trust's own figures twice and has a page of notes on the motion. Nobody else in the room seems to have read them at all.
scene B: Half the room has drifted home. Whoever is still here at ten will decide what the branch says about the hospital, and {N} means to be here.
scene R: The maternity ward is closing, and the room is arguing about whether the agenda went out on time. {N} can feel the words building up like steam.
scene G: The same faces sit in the same chairs as every month: {colleague} by the radiator, the old couple by the door. The branch has met above this pub for forty years.
scene tribal: The fire has burned down twice, and the faction's elders are still on the old quarrel about the eastern hearths. The matter of the healer's lodge, the one {N} came to hear, has not been raised.
scene magic: The candles in the back room of the guild hall are half gone, and the faction's warden is still reading last month's petitions aloud. The motion on the infirmary waits at the bottom of the roll.
- W | learn the standing orders, and use them to move the hospital motion up | v: conformity, achievement | grants: knowing the rules of the house | tribal: learn the customs of the fire, and use them to bring the healer's matter forward | chance: 70
- U | read the trust's own figures to the room before anyone votes | identity | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: tell the fire what the healer has seen this winter, before anyone decides | magic: read the infirmary's own ledgers to the room before anyone votes | chance: 70
- B | stay until the room empties, and carry the motion with whoever is left | v: power, achievement | self_control: + | chance: 90
- R | stand up out of turn and say what the closure means to the street | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: defied an authority | tribal: speak out of turn and say what losing the healer means to every hearth | chance: 35
- G | stay quiet, as the old members do, and vote with the room when it comes | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 95
- W>R | propose a five-minute limit on every speech, so the branch gets to act | v: self-direction | tribal: ask the elders to let each voice speak once, so the fire gets to decide | chance: 65
- U>G | write the hospital's story for the branch newsletter, so members know what is at stake | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: gather what the old ones remember of the healer's lodge, and tell it at the next fire | magic: write the infirmary's story for the faction's broadsheet, so members know what is at stake | chance: 90
- B>W | plan to stand for branch chair next year, and run every meeting to time | v: power | aims: local party officer | tribal: plan to ask the families to make you an elder of the faction, and keep its fires short | magic: plan to stand for the faction's ward office next year, and run its meetings to time | chance: 75
- R>U | walk out, and go and ask the night-shift nurses what is really happening | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: walk away from the fire, and go and ask the healer herself | magic: walk out, and go and ask the infirmary's night nurses yourself | chance: 65
- G>B | have a quiet word with the old members by the door, and collect their proxy votes | v: power, tradition | closed: approval: proxy votes the branch rules do not allow; backfire: the votes are struck out, and the chair notes it in the minutes | tribal: have a quiet word with the old families, and speak in their name once they have gone to sleep | magic: have a quiet word with the old members, and cast their lots for them | chance: 65
works: The motion is reached at last, and the branch says what it came to say.
works: {N} walks home late and tired, sure that the evening counted for something.
fails: Time runs out, and the motion is put off until next month, again.
fails: The vote goes the other way, and {boss} thanks {N} a little too politely at the door.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 4 options (check.py section 4).
== the party changes its line
tier: everyday
rate: 0.001
gap: 5-10
times: per party member a year: about 1 in 10 see the leadership drop or reverse a promise they joined for; it comes with most new leaders and most manifestos (estimate)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 16-95
calls: URG
holds: party member
tone: trouble
life: community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, friend
earth: a policy shift announced on the news: the leadership drops the promise you joined the party for
tribal: the faction makes peace with the family it swore against, and the oath you took against them is set aside
magic: the faction takes the Order's side in the Assembly, against the cause you swore to it for
scene: The six o'clock news carries it in one line: the party is dropping the promise {N} joined for. By seven {Ns} phone is full of messages from the branch, most of them in capital letters.
scene W: {N} signed the membership form because of that promise, and still believes a promise made to voters should be kept. The party's own rules say policy is made by conference, not by a press release.
scene U: {N} reads the new policy paper in full, twice, looking for what changed and why. Some of the reasoning holds up, and that is almost worse.
scene B: The leadership has chosen, and from now on the members who matter will be the ones who back it. {N} can already see which way the next selection will go.
scene R: {N} remembers the night of joining: the hall, the cheering, the promise said out loud. Now it is gone in a line on the news, and {N} is shaking with it.
scene G: {Ns} family has voted for this party for three generations. One bad policy does not undo a family, {N} tells {friend}, not quite believing it.
scene tribal: The faction's elders have shared a fire with the family the band swore against, and the old oath is set aside. {N} swore that oath too, at the same fire, three winters ago.
scene magic: The heralds cry it in the square at noon: the faction will stand with the Order in the Assembly. {N} swore the faction's oath for the opposite cause.
- W | put a motion to the next conference to restore the promise, by the rules | v: conformity, universalism | tribal: ask the elders to hear the oath again at the next great fire | magic: put a motion to the faction's next assembly to restore the cause | chance: 55
- U | write a paper on what dropping the promise will cost, and send it up the line | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: work out what the peace will cost the band, and take it to the elders | chance: 55
- B | back the new line in public, and ask for a policy seat in return | identity | v: power, achievement | tribal: speak for the peace at the fire, and ask for a place among the faction's elders in return | magic: back the faction's new line in the square, and ask for a seat in its inner council | chance: 70
- R | tear up your membership card, and say why in public | v: self-direction | identity | drops: party member | tribal: walk away from the faction's fire, and say why before them all | magic: break your faction seal in the square, and say why | chance: 95
- G | stay in and stay quiet; parties change, and the old members outlast them | v: tradition | self_control: + | tribal: stay with the faction and say nothing; factions change, and the old families outlast them | magic: stay in the faction and say nothing; factions change, and the old houses outlast them | chance: 80
- W>R | sign the rebels' open letter, with your name and your branch on it | v: stimulation, universalism | identity | mark: defied an authority | tribal: stand with the young ones who refuse the peace, and say your name aloud | magic: put your seal to the rebels' open letter, with your ward beside it | chance: 95
- U>G | dig out the party's founding statement, and show the branch the promise was always there | door | v: tradition | tribal: remind the families of the words said when the faction first swore the oath | magic: find the faction's founding charter, and show the ward the cause was always there | chance: 70
- B>W | trade your support for a written pledge on the one local promise that matters | v: power | binds | tribal: trade your silence for the elders' word on the one thing your hearths need | magic: trade your support for a sealed pledge on the one local promise that matters | chance: 50
- R>U | go to the rebels' weekend meeting on a whim, to see who they really are | v: stimulation, self-direction | door | tribal: go to the young ones' fire on a whim, to see who they really are | chance: 90
- G>B | ride it out, and keep the branch ready for the day the leadership falls | v: power | tribal: wait it out, and keep your hearths ready for the day the faction head falls | magic: ride it out, and keep the ward ready for the day the faction's head falls | chance: 45
works: The storm passes, and {N} knows where {N} stands with the party.
works: {N} finds others who feel the same, and the anger turns into a plan.
fails: Nothing {N} does moves the leadership an inch, and the branch splits over it anyway.
fails: {friend} hears what {N} said, and keeps away for a month.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- local councillor: calls UR / calls WBG (cycle s4)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
== the planning committee and the field
tier: everyday
rate: 0.015
gap: 5-10
times: per local councillor a year: a contested planning vote comes several times a year, a big one on a green field every few years; about 1 life in 100 sits on a council (catalogue share; about 117,000 councillors in England, LGIU and NALC)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
calls: UR
holds: local councillor
tone: trouble
life: community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, rival
earth: the planning application for the last green field in the ward comes to committee: two hundred houses, the officers for it, and a hall full of residents against it
tribal: the band wants to move camp onto the old burial ground by the river, and every voice at the fire must say where it stands
magic: a guild wants the common meadow outside the walls for its new hall, and the town council must vote on the grant
scene: The application is for two hundred houses on the last open field in the ward. The officers recommend approval, the party group wants it through, and the public seats are full of residents with home-made placards.
scene W: The rules are clear: a councillor decides a planning case on planning grounds, not on who shouts loudest. {N} also promised the ward a fair hearing, and meant it.
scene U: {N} has read all four hundred pages, the flood-risk appendix included. Page three hundred and twelve does not say what the summary says it says.
scene B: {boss}, who leads the group, has made it plain that the council needs the houses and the money that comes with them. A vote against would be remembered at the next reshuffle of committee seats.
scene R: {N} grew up kicking a ball on that field. Looking at the placards, {N} feels the whole room tilt toward one answer.
scene G: The field has been grazed, played on and walked across for as long as anyone can remember. Two hundred families also need somewhere to live.
scene tribal: The elders point at the riverbank where the old ones lie: the driest ground for the winter camp, and the most sacred. Every voice at the fire is asked where it stands, and {N} is next.
scene magic: The guild's masters have laid the drawings for their new hall on the council table. Outside the window, the common meadow where the town's goats graze is gold in the evening light.
- W | vote with the officers' advice, on planning grounds alone | v: conformity, security | tribal: follow the custom the eldest set out, and nothing more | chance: 90
- U | find the flaw in the flood report, and ask for the decision to be put off | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: show the fire where the river rose in the old floods, and ask the band to wait | magic: find the flaw in the guild's survey of the meadow, and ask for the vote to be put off | chance: 45
- B | vote it through, and get the developer to pay for a sports hall for the ward | v: power, achievement | tribal: agree to the move, if your own hearths get the driest ground | magic: vote it through, and make the guild pay for a new well in the ward | chance: 55
- R | stand with the residents and vote against, whatever the group says | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: defied an authority | tribal: stand with the families against the move, whatever the elders say | magic: stand with the townsfolk and vote against, whatever the faction says | chance: 30
- G | speak for the field as common ground the ward has always shared | identity | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: speak for the dead who lie there, and the ground the band has always kept | magic: speak for the meadow as common ground the town has always shared | chance: 35
- W>G | propose a rule that protects the ward's last open spaces, and take it to full council | v: universalism, security | identity | grants: a reform with your name on it | tribal: propose a custom that no camp is ever made on the resting places of the dead | magic: propose a bylaw that keeps the town's commons common, and take it to the full council | chance: 15
- U>W | broker a compromise the residents, the officers and the group can all sign | binds | v: universalism, conformity | grants: coalition building | tribal: work out a camp the families, the elders and the young can all accept | magic: broker a compromise the townsfolk, the guild and the council can all seal | chance: 55
- B>U | ask a friend in the trade what the developer's figures really show | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask a friend what the elders really said in private | magic: ask a friend in the guild what its ledgers really show | chance: 65
- R>B | make a fiery speech against it, with the local paper's photographer in the room | v: power, stimulation | tribal: make a fiery speech against it, with the singers listening | magic: make a fiery speech against it, with the broadsheet writers in the room | chance: 75
- G>R | walk the field with the residents on Sunday, and let the anger become a campaign | binds | v: benevolence, stimulation | body: light | tribal: walk the burial ground with the families at dawn, and let the anger rise | magic: walk the meadow with the townsfolk on the feast day, and let the anger become a campaign | chance: 70
works: The committee reaches a decision {N} can stand behind, and the ward hears why.
works: The vote is closer than anyone expected, and the field's future is settled for a few years at least.
fails: The vote goes the other way, and the people in the public seats leave without looking at {N}.
fails: {boss} hears how {N} handled it, and {N} is off the committee by the spring.

== a knock at the door at ten at night
tier: everyday
rate: 0.015
gap: 5-10
times: per local councillor a year: most councillors are approached at home by a resident in trouble a few times a year (estimate)
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
calls: WBG
holds: local councillor
tone: trouble
life: community, home
horizon: moment
roles: partner, a resident
earth: at ten at night a resident you have never met is on your doorstep: her flat is flooding from upstairs, and the landlord will not answer
tribal: a family comes to your hearth at night with a feud that will not wait for the council fire
magic: a petitioner knocks at your door after the curfew bell, with a grievance against the guild that cannot wait until morning
scene: At ten at night the doorbell goes. A woman {N} has never met stands on the step in a wet coat: water is pouring through her ceiling, and the landlord's emergency line has rung out for an hour.
scene W: {N} stood for the council to serve the ward, and the ward does not keep office hours. There is also a proper way to log a case, and a reason for it.
scene B: {partner} is calling from the kitchen that dinner is going cold. The woman on the step lives in a street that never votes for {N}, and the thought arrives before {N} can stop it.
scene G: {N} knows her block: built two generations ago, with pipes older than most of its tenants. {N} knows the caretaker by name too.
scene U: {N} has seen this landlord's name on a dozen cases before. Somewhere in its tenancy agreement is a clause it would rather nobody read.
scene R: She is shaking, from the cold or from the evening she has had. {N} wants to grab a coat and go, and also wants, badly, one evening off.
scene tribal: Long after dark a family stands at the edge of {Ns} firelight. A quarrel over a stolen net has come to blows, and they will not wait for the council fire to hear it.
scene magic: After the curfew bell there is a lamp at the door and a frightened face behind it: a tanner whose workshop the guild has sealed, with a petition and nowhere else to take it.
- W | take her details, and log the case with the council's emergency line | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: hear them out, and promise the matter to the elders at first light | magic: take the petition, and enter it in the council's night book | chance: 90
- U | find the clause in her tenancy that binds the landlord, and coach her through the call | v: self-direction, benevolence | tribal: work out which old custom settles a stolen net, and tell them what to say | magic: find the clause in the guild's charter that covers her, and coach her through it | chance: 85
- B | ring the housing director's private number, the one only councillors have | v: power, benevolence | tribal: wake the head of the other family yourself, and press them to settle | magic: send for the guild master by name, a summons only councillors may send | chance: 90
- R | tell her this is not the time, and to come to the surgery on Saturday | v: hedonism, security | mark: refused someone in need | self_control: - | tribal: send them away from your fire until the council meets | magic: tell the petitioner to come back at the proper hour | chance: 95
- G | go round with her, and knock on the upstairs neighbour's door yourself | v: benevolence | body: light | mark: helped someone in need | tribal: walk with them to the other family's hearth, and sit down between them | magic: go with the petitioner to the guild hall's door yourself | chance: 75
- W>G | set up an evening surgery on her estate, so people need not come to your door | v: benevolence, security | binds | tribal: set a night each moon when families may bring their quarrels to your hearth | magic: set a weekly evening for petitions in the ward's own hall | chance: 80
- U>W | work the case through properly, and write it up for the housing committee | v: universalism, conformity | grants: constituency casework | tribal: work the quarrel through, and bring it to the council fire plainly set out | magic: take the case through the proper offices, and write it up for the council | chance: 90
- B>U | use the case to make the landlord hand over its repair records | v: power, achievement | tribal: use the quarrel to make both families say what really happened | magic: use the case to make the guild open its books on the sealed workshops | chance: 65
- R>B | post her story online tonight, with your name at the front of the fight | v: power, stimulation | closed: approval: a resident's trouble made public without her leave; backfire: she complains to the council's standards officer | tribal: tell the quarrel loudly at the next fire, with your name at the front of it | magic: cry her grievance in the square, with your name at the front of the fight | chance: 75
- G>R | take her in for the night, and let the case wait until morning | v: benevolence | tribal: take the family in at your own fire for the night | magic: take the petitioner in for the night, and let the guild wait until morning | chance: 95
works: The worst is over before midnight, and the case is in hand by morning.
works: A week later the resident tells the neighbours who came to the door, and what happened next.
fails: Nothing anyone does tonight helps, and by morning the damage is done.
fails: {partner} goes to bed without a word, and the evening is gone for nothing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- campaign organiser: calls BG / calls WUR (cycle s1)

== forty volunteers and one weekend left
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per campaign organiser: once or twice a campaign the volunteers and the days run short of the target; about 1 life in 500 works as a paid organiser (catalogue share; estimate)
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-70
calls: BG
holds: campaign organiser
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague
earth: a campaign office with a whiteboard of streets: forty volunteers, one weekend left, and the target lists say the ward is lost
tribal: a dozen young hunters to send to the far bands before the gathering, and the word from the hearths is that the speaker will lose
magic: forty runners and a single day before the lots are cast, and the tallies say the faction's candidate is beaten
scene: Forty volunteers, one weekend and a whiteboard of streets. The target lists say the ward is lost by about two hundred votes, and {boss}, the candidate, has not been told yet.
scene W: The volunteers gave up their weekend on {Ns} word that it would count. {N} owes them a plan that does.
scene U: {N} pulls up the turnout from the last three elections and starts looking for the streets where the model might be wrong.
scene B: The regional office wants the forty sent to the next ward, where the race is close. A win there would look very good on {Ns} record.
scene R: {boss} has knocked on doors every night for six weeks. {N} cannot bring the news, not yet and not like this.
scene G: {N} knows these streets: which families talk to which, which estate turns out late, which pensioners still remember the candidate's mother.
scene tribal: A dozen young hunters wait at the edge of the camp with packs on their backs. The word from the hearths is plain: the band's speaker will lose at the gathering, unless something changes before the moon is full.
scene magic: Forty runners crowd the faction's hall at dawn, waiting for orders. The tallies say the lots will go against the faction's candidate tomorrow.
- W | tell the volunteers the truth about the numbers, and let them choose where to go | v: universalism, benevolence | tribal: tell the young hunters what the hearths are saying, and let them choose where to go | magic: tell the runners what the tallies say, and let them choose where to go | chance: 65
- U | find the streets the model got wrong, and send everyone there | v: achievement | mark: learned a skill | tribal: find the hearths the elders have misjudged, and send everyone there | magic: find the lanes the tallies got wrong, and send everyone there | chance: 45
- B | send the forty to the close ward next door, where a win is still possible | v: achievement, power | tribal: send the hunters to the bands still undecided, where it can still be won | magic: send the runners to the next ward, where the lots are still close | chance: 70
- R | throw everyone at the ward anyway, in one last loud weekend | v: stimulation, self-direction | mark: took a wild risk | tribal: send everyone to the far bands anyway, singing, in one last loud push | magic: throw every runner at the ward anyway, in one last loud day | chance: 30
- G | send them to the streets you know will turn out if someone knocks twice | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: send them to the hearths you know will stand up if asked twice | magic: send them to the lanes you know will turn out if someone knocks twice | chance: 70
- W>U | log every door this weekend, so the next campaign knows the ward | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: mark every hearth visited, so the next speaker knows the band | magic: keep a roll of every door this day, so the next campaign knows the ward | chance: 70
- U>B | send the region inflated canvass returns, so it backs the ward next time | v: power, achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: false canvass figures sent to the party; backfire: the count shows the gap, and the region stops trusting the figures | tribal: tell the faction head more hearths stand with the speaker than really do | magic: send the faction's house inflated tallies, so it backs the ward next time | chance: 85
- B>R | promise the volunteers a party at your own expense if they double the doors | v: hedonism, achievement | tribal: promise the hunters a feast from your own stores if they reach every band | magic: promise the runners a night at the tavern on your purse if they double the doors | chance: 90
- R>G | knock doors all weekend yourself, beside the candidate's oldest friends | v: benevolence, tradition | body: light | tribal: walk to the far hearths yourself, beside the speaker's oldest kin | self_control: + | chance: 90
- G>W | keep the volunteers on the home ward, as you promised the branch | v: tradition, conformity | mark: kept your word | tribal: keep the hunters among the band's own hearths, as you promised the elders | magic: keep the runners in the home ward, as you promised the faction | chance: 85
works: The weekend's work shows in the count, closer than the lists ever said.
works: The helpers go home at the end tired, proud and asking about the next campaign.
fails: The lists were right, and the count is as bad as they said.
fails: Half the helpers drift off by the second afternoon, and the plan falls apart.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== the candidate goes off script
tier: everyday
rate: 0.5
times: per campaign organiser: about one campaign in three has a candidate's slip that spreads (estimate)
stages: young_adult adult mature
age: 18-70
calls: WUR
holds: campaign organiser
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, colleague, rival
earth: at noon the candidate said something careless on camera, and by two the clip is on every phone in the ward
tribal: the speaker insulted a rival clan's elder in front of the gathering's runners, and the singers have the line already
magic: the candidate mocked the Order in the market square, and the broadsheet writers took it down word for word
scene: At a market stall at noon {boss}, the candidate, made a careless joke about the people of the next town. By two the clip is on every phone in the ward, and {rival} has shared it twice.
scene W: There are nine days left and a code of conduct the campaign signed in public. {N} reads it again with the clip playing on mute.
scene U: {N} watches the clip frame by frame. Cut ten seconds earlier it means something else, and the other side has cut it ten seconds earlier.
scene B: A good organiser can turn a bad day into a story about the other side's dirty tricks. {N} has done it before.
scene R: {N} wants to throw the phone across the office, then drive to wherever {boss} is and have it out face to face.
scene G: The town the joke was about is where half the volunteers' families come from. {N} can already hear what will be said at the school gates.
scene tribal: At the trading place {boss}, the band's speaker, mocked an elder of the river clan in front of runners from three bands. By dusk the singers are already making it a song.
scene magic: In the market square {boss} made a jest at the Order's expense, before a crowd and two broadsheet writers. By the evening bell the words are pasted on the tavern doors.
- W | get the candidate to apologise in plain words before the evening news | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: owned up | tribal: bring the speaker to the elder's hearth to say sorry before dusk | magic: have the candidate apologise in plain words before the evening bell | chance: 50
- U | put out the full, uncut clip, so people can judge the words in context | v: universalism, achievement | grants: handling the press | tribal: get the runners who were there to tell the whole of what was said | magic: send the heralds the whole of what was said, word for word | chance: 70
- B | say the clip was doctored by the other side | v: power | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a false claim that the clip was faked; backfire: the full recording surfaces, and the story doubles | tribal: say the rival clan's runners twisted the speaker's words | magic: say the broadsheet writers twisted the candidate's words | chance: 40
- R | quit on the spot, rather than defend that | v: self-direction | drops: campaign organiser | chance: 95
